{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 4880,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a two dimensional version of our universe, \nelectrons would still orbit atoms and there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8400,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would still be a periodic table for elements, \nbut it would be different from our table and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11920,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would look like this. Let’s explore why.\nThis is oversimplifying, but the chemical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16080,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "properties of an atom are determined by \nhow its outermost electrons are behaving,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19240,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "since they're the most weakly attracted to \nthe nucleus and therefore most available to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22640,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interact with other atoms. So, roughly speaking, \nthe periodic table is a diagram of which orbital  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27200,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happens to be the outermost one for each atom.\nIn 3D, the possible orbitals of an electron have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31440,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energies that look something like this - left to \nright means orbiting with more angular momentum,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35520,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and bottom to top means orbiting with more \nenergy (and generally more distance). When  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39120,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you throw a bunch of electrons around a nucleus, \nthey \"fill up\" the possible orbitals (and don't  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43120,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "share them more than two per orbital because \nelectrons don't like each other ) kind of like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46520,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "water filling up a funny-shaped tank. So which \norbital the outermost electron is in depends  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50640,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on both the shape of the tank and how full you \nfill it - that is, how many electrons there are."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66560,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When you gradually fill up the tank to see \nwhat the outermost orbital of each element is,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70280,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you get our periodic table. Well, \nafter a slight rearrangement "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73440,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For 2D atoms, there are two big differences:\nFirst, while in 3D the electromagnetic force that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78200,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attracts electrons to the nucleus is a 1/r2 law \n(since it depends on the surface area of a sphere  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82600,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "), in 2D a sphere is just a circle whose \"surface \narea\" is its circumference, so the electromagnetic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87920,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "force is a 1/r law; this difference changes \nthe vertical spacings of the energy levels. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92360,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And second, because in 2D there are \nfewer dimensions in which to move,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95600,
    "dDurationMs": 3272,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are also fewer ways for electrons to orbit, \nwhich means fewer orbits for each energy level ,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98872,
    "dDurationMs": 1928,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which looks like fewer horizontal spaces in the \nenergy levels. In particular, for a given amount  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100800,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of angular momentum, an electron can only be \norbiting clockwise or counterclockwise (unlike 3D  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105600,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where there are a lot more possible orientations), \nso for each value of angular momentum there are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109960,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only two possible orbitals at each energy level. \nExcept for the lone zero angular momentum orbital,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114400,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which isn't going clockwise or counterclockwise.\nIf we gradually fill up the \"tank\" of 2D orbitals  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118280,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with electrons to see what the outermost orbit of \neach element is, the result is a table that looks  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122280,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like this - or, after a slight rearrangement, \nsomething more like the typical table for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126160,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our 3D universe. But it's definitely different.\nThere are two ways we might name the 2D elements:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131080,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We could use the same names as the 3D ones with \nthe same number of protons and electrons, so that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135280,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Carbon, Sulfur, Zinc, and Cadmium would be the \nnames of the 2D noble gases, and the 2D halogens  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140280,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "(which in 3D are fluorine, chlorine, etc) would \nbe instead Boron, Phosphorus, Copper, Silver, etc.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145880,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other proposal is to name 2D elements based \non the equivalent chemical properties, so that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the noble gases are still Neon, Argon, Krypton, \netc, the halogens are still fluorine and chlorine,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154760,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so on. This second naming proposal means we \nleave out a lot of names, but we also get to add  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159120,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some new ones because there are blocks in the 2D \nperiodic table that we don't have in the 3D one! "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164160,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Is a 2D periodic table useful? I don’t know. \nMaybe it could help predict the behaviors of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167600,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quasiparticles on a 2D surface like a thin \nlayer of graphene, maybe it can help improve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171320,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mathematical techniques, but mostly I think \nit's an exercise in curiosity. It's simply  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175240,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cool that we can calculate and predict the \nwhole 2D periodic table (and have confidence  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179000,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that it's reasonably accurate since the same type \nof calculations correctly predict the 3D table),  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182960,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even if we could never actually experience a 2D \nuniverse. And any differences and similarities we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187000,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "find can help show us/help us see what's unique \nabout the 3D periodic table and what's not."
    } ]
  } ]
}
